I fixed the problem the evaluator found: on Unix, StreamingBashTool now gives bash an empty stdin (/dev/null) as well as its own process group. Before this, a command that reads input in an interactive session (read, sudo, ssh, git credential prompts) froze silently until the timeout. Build, the full cargo test run (0 failures), clippy, fmt and tests/harness_logic.sh all pass, and it's committed as 3a621e67.

What changed
• **`src/tools.rs** (+5 lines): std_cmd.stdin(Stdio::null()) is set next to process_group(0), only on Unix. This matches yoagent 0.25.2's BashTool (src/tools/bash.rs:190-192`). A command that reads input now gets end-of-file straight away and never sees your terminal. Windows behaviour is unchanged.
• **New test** in src/tools_group_kill_tests.rs, test_streaming_bash_stdin_is_null_so_readers_get_eof:
  • `if read -r x; then …; else echo eof; fi must output exactly Exit code: 0\neof`.
  • On Linux, readlink /proc/$$/fd/0 must output exactly /dev/null.
• **Positive control:** I commented out the stdin line with a `NEUTERED` marker, ran the tests, and restored it, all in one command. The new test failed by name, because stdin was an inherited pipe:[…] instead of /dev/null. After restoring, it passes and no marker is left in the file.
• **`tests/module_size.rs:** the size limit for src/tools.rs` goes from 5202 to 5207 lines, with a note on what the extra 5 lines are.
• **`ARCHITECTURE.md:** a dated paragraph under the src/tools.rs` entry covering what changed, why, the reference it copies, the test and its control.

Limitations of the test:
• During cargo test stdin is not a terminal, so the test can't reproduce the freeze itself. It checks the cause (stdin was inherited) rather than the symptom; you reproduced the symptom in a pty.
• The read half passed even with the fix removed. Only the /proc check failed, so on macOS and other non-Linux Unix the test has no part that would catch this.
